{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310855,"nonce":"Uhf/qw9SakoPsEUNR+UWNWefaI8=","title":"Pending optimization + correctness work after the 2026-06 compiler-arc session. Tree is at b013900 with the trap-stub cascade closed for ...","message":"- **Symptom**: rayzor llama-chat with prompts that produce long outputs (\"Write a linked list implementation in rust\", \"Write a sonnet\" at N=100) crash with the OS killing the process around ~30GB resident.\n- **Hypothesis** (high confidence): `InsertFreePass` misses an escape root in the decode loop — per-step intermediates (post-RoPE K/V views, attention scores, FFN intermediates) accumulate instead of being freed. Same family as [[bugs-mark-escaping-incomplete]] which was fixed for `Field/Index/Call` arg paths; another path (likely the closure-captured tensor inside `onToken` callback or the iface-method-return intermediate) still leaks.\n- **Discriminative probe**: add `static AtomicUsize` counters around `rayzor_tensor_alloc` + `rayzor_tensor_free` in [runtime/src/tensor.rs](runtime/src/tensor.rs); print delta per N decode steps. Linear growth → leak confirmed; bounded → memory math wrong elsewhere.\n- **Effort**: ~50 LOC instrumentation + 1 verify run. Fix likely ~30-100 LOC in `drop_analysis.rs`.\n- **Starter**: instrument, run \"Write a sonnet\" with 50 tokens, plot allocs vs step.\n\n- **Symptom**: ~1/10 cold canonical, ~2/10 cold short prompt SIGTRAP at `[gen] streaming...`. Content-dependent (sky prompt 8/10, test 6/10, Paris 2/10).\n- **Ruled out** this session: worker_pool race, tier-2 swap mid-decode, trap-stub leak past Path E, runtime panic.\n- **Remaining candidates** (per [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]]):\n  - H1: RoPE half-split emits NaN at certain `positionOffset` values\n  - H2: `expandKvHeads` broadcast view stride=0 reads out-of-range KV rows\n  - H3: attention mask construction OOB at seq_pos=1\n- **Discriminative probe**: GQAttention `trace()` at pre-append + pre-bmm with shape/strides/finite-count. **MUST** scrub `.rayzor` + `cargo clean` first or the trace lines never fire (BLADE cache replays stale code).\n- **Effort**: ~1-2 workflows wall (~30-60 min).\n- **Starter**: `find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +; cargo clean; cargo build --release` then re-apply the `trace()` patch and replay.\n\n- **Symptom**: 20+ `Error: [IMPORT[X]] Cannot find name / Cannot access field` lines print on every llama-chat startup despite the code running fine.\n- **Root cause**: live import resolver emits diagnostic before MIR cache check completes; cache catches the resolution, runtime works, errors are spurious.\n- **One attempted fix this session**: pre-register all abstract members in `ast_lowering.rs` before lowering bodies. Silenced all 20+ errors AND llama-chat still produced Paris answer — **but** broke tiny-transformer with SIGSEGV. Reverted.\n- **Real fix paths** (pick one):\n  - (a) Suppress diagnostic when MIR cache resolves the access (in the import resolver emission site)\n  - (b) Narrow the pre-register patch to only stdlib abstracts (Int32/Int64/FPHelper/BytesBuffer, not user abstracts)\n  - (c) Investigate why pre-registration breaks tiny-transformer; targeted fix to keep both\n- **Effort**: (a) smallest; (b) medium; (c) largest.\n- **Starter**: grep for `imported file` format string in `compiler/src/` to locate emission site; gate on a cache-hit flag.\n\n\n- **Context**: `matmul_qt_threaded` is 72% of decode wall post-[[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]]. Q6_K SDOT was tried, gave -4.7% because per-block 6-bit reconstruction overhead (4 shift/mask pairs per 16-weight span × 8 spans = 32 instructions just to unpack) eats the SDOT density win.\n- **Real fix**: 2×2 tile that processes multiple Q6_K blocks per X-block load, amortising reconstruction across multiple output rows. See [runtime/src/quant.rs:2591-2598](runtime/src/quant.rs#L2591) original author's note.\n- **Estimated win**: hard to forecast without prototyping. Plausibly 1.3-1.8× on the lm_head path; ~20-40% total decode wall.\n- **Effort**: ~150-200 LOC NEON SIMD.\n- **Starter**: prototype the inner tile in a Rust standalone (out-of-tree), benchmark vs Q6_K fallback, port if it wins.\n\n- **Alternative to (4)**: re-quantize the Q6_K lm_head/embed to Q4_K_M at load, letting them join the working SDOT path. Requires the F32→Q4_K_M encoder that prior workflow `w3710qy2i` descoped.\n- **Estimated win**: similar to (4), perhaps cleaner. Trade-off: tiny extra quantization loss on greedy temp=0.01.\n- **Effort**: ~250-300 LOC port of `make_qkx2_quants` from llama.cpp.\n- **Starter**: port the encoder, gate behind `RAYZOR_REQUANT_LM_HEAD=1`, MATCH-check on canonical prompt.\n\n- Per [discussion this session]: Cranelift FP is conservative; Haxe-side RoPE rotation, RMSNorm reduce, softmax `exp` would benefit from `Fma` fusion + reassoc. Most rayzor hot kernels are already in Rust+NEON intrinsics so this doesn't move the dominant 72% matmul wall, but ~1-5% per call site adds up over RoPE + RMSNorm + softmax per layer per token.\n- **Effort**: Cranelift path = peephole `Fadd(Fmul(a,b),c) → Fma(a,b,c)` in MIR lowering (~50 LOC). LLVM tier-3 path = `builder.set_fast_math_flags(FastMathFlags::all())` per function (~10 LOC, gated by `llvm-backend` feature).\n- **Starter**: pick Cranelift path; add peephole in `compiler/src/ir/mir_to_cranelift.rs` (or wherever); bench RMSNorm.\n\n- Per [[project-wasm-phase2]]: WASM Tensor `_start` arity mismatch (offset 0x13db7+) and integer SIMD (i8x16/i16x8/i32x4 splat/binop/reduce) needed for INT8/INT4 quant kernels on WASM target.\n- **Effort**: ~200-400 LOC in `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs`.\n- **Starter**: fix `_start` arity first, then add per-dtype SIMD lowering.\n\n\n- Per [[project-numa-followups]]: NumaPool + NumaTopology in pure Haxe (per [[feedback-numa-pure-haxe]]). Compiler fixes needed: Send for Function, multi-arg arrow bug, Ptr\u003cT\u003e, spawn return-type generic, channel close, singleton sugar.\n- **Multi-socket Linux only**; macOS M1 Pro single-socket gives nothing here. Skip until target hardware available.\n\n- Per [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] residual: IrFunctionId(280007/220015) in BertArch.build / LlamaArch.build / GGUFTokenizer.build. Track B attempted, no-change.\n- **Only needed if** a future use case actually hits this — current llama-chat works at 9/10 cold-clean despite the warning.\n- **Effort if pursued**: instrument `user_function_name_map` dump pre-fixup to discriminate seed-ordering vs `qualified_name=None`; targeted fix.\n\n\nFor perf-first goal: **#1 (tensor leak)** → **#4 (Q6_K 2×2 tile)** → **#2 (SIGTRAP)** → **#6 (fast-math)**.\n\nFor correctness-first goal: **#1 (tensor leak)** → **#2 (SIGTRAP)** → **#3 (import noise)** → perf.\n\n\n\n**Commit `52496c3` — `Tensor.getFlat(i:Int):Float`** — first profile-driven optimization using the new SIGPROF infra (`tools/profile_run.sh`).\n\n- Profile showed `LocalTempSampler.sample` at Main.hx:66 dominated Haxe attribution: 5721 samples over 20s = ~14% of decode wall.\n- Root cause: the 128k-vocab top-K scan called `logits.get([i])` once per iteration. Each call allocated a Haxe `Array\u003cInt\u003e` for `[i]` then crossed FFI — 128k allocs per generated token, all size 16 bytes, all immediately freed.\n- Fix: add `Tensor.getFlat(i:Int):Float` extern that skips the indexing path; contiguous-tensor fast path is `data[i * dtype_size]`. Strided fallback for views. ~50 LOC total across runtime + compiler stdlib + Haxe binding.\n- **Measured: 12-13 → 20.5 tok/s on nue/llama-chat (M1 Pro, warm cache, 80-token \"Once upon a time\" prompt). +58% tok/s.**\n- MATCH preserved: canonical \"What is the capital of France?\" → \"The capital of France is Paris.\" byte-exact.\n\nThis demonstrates the full workflow: instrument → profile → identify Haxe hot spot → fix at the right layer (runtime FFI surface) → measure delta → commit.\n\n\nAfter 52496c3 landed, attempting to re-profile to find the NEW dominant hot spot hits the known content-dependent SIGTRAP per [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] mid-decode. Capture is incomplete for the post-getFlat state. Workaround: arm SIGPROF only around a specific safe region via the `rayzor_profile_start`/`stop` FFI from Haxe (commit 89ad22f) and skip the trap-prone window. Or: fix the runtime SIGTRAP (Tier 1 #2) before further profile-driven optimization.\n\n\nAfter the getFlat sweep, the realistic next perf targets:\n\n\n- **Defensive clones in LlamaModel.forwardIds** ([nue/nue/arch/LlamaModel.hx:71](nue/nue/arch/LlamaModel.hx#L71)): `var hIn = h.clone(); h = block.forward(hIn);` runs once per layer (16× per token). Each clone is ~8 KB (hidden state). The comment notes this is a workaround for an analyzer false-positive + a downstream runtime SIGSEGV when the clone is removed. Fix path is in the compiler's `drop_analysis` / cross-file `@:move` checker, not nue. Probably ~5% wall if removed cleanly. Same workaround at line 74 for `outputNorm.forward(hNormIn)`.\n\n- **`model.forwardIds([nextId])` allocates a fresh `Array\u003cInt\u003e` per decode step**. Replace with a pre-allocated reusable `Array\u003cInt\u003e` of length 1 (mutate the [0] element). Tiny per-step alloc removed, microsecond-scale.\n\n\n- **Q6_K SDOT 2×2 tile** ([[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]]) — the largest documented unrealized win: 1.3-1.8× on the lm_head path (~20-40% total decode wall). ~150-200 LOC of NEON SIMD work in [runtime/src/quant.rs:2590-2605](runtime/src/quant.rs#L2590). Inner kernel structure: instead of `for output_row { for block { decode → dot } }`, emit `for output_row in 2x2 tile { for block { decode_two_rows; dot_both_with_shared_x_chunk } }`. The win comes from amortising per-block scale/bias lookups + x_chunk cache locality.\n\n- **F32→Q4_K_M re-quantize lm_head at GGUF load** (alternative to ↑) — port `make_qkx2_quants` from llama.cpp (~250-300 LOC). Lets `lm_head` join the working Q4_K_M SDOT path instead of staying on Q6_K. Numerically slightly lossier but greedy decode at temp=0.01 should tolerate it. Verify MATCH against llama.cpp.\n\n\n- **Manual FMA fusion in MIR→Cranelift lowering** — Cranelift's egraph rules canonicalize `fma(-x, -y, z) → fma(x, y, z)` but don't fuse `fadd(fmul(a,b), c) → fma(a,b,c)`. For Haxe-side RMSNorm reduce, RoPE rotation, softmax — would need ~50 LOC peephole in `cranelift_backend.rs` lowering. Small per-call-site win but adds up across per-token compute.\n\n- **Egraph rule for fadd+fmul fusion in the patched cranelift fork** — alternative to manual peephole. Adds a rule to `cranelift/codegen/src/opts/arithmetic.isle` then rebuilds the fork. Cleaner architecturally but requires forking maintenance.\n\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] — historical investigation; this session closed the compiler half\n- [[bugs-q6k-sdot-no-win]] — original Q6_K SDOT result + 2×2 tile path forward\n- [[bugs-mark-escaping-incomplete]] — escape-analysis fix template for #1\n- [[project-llama-chat-coherence-phase-c]] — bmm stride-awareness fix that may have left a gap reachable by H2\n- [[project-session-2026-06-compiler-arc]] — this session's compiler-fix arc summary\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_optimization_roadmap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}